# Sending Raw HTTP Requests Without Encoding in PHP

When you need to transmit a string exactly as it existsâwithout `urlencode()`, JSON serialization, or any automatic encodingâyou are dealing with a raw HTTP POST. The goal is to send the payload directly to a server expecting `Content-Type: text/plain` (or similar) and a specific `Content-Length`. In PHP, using cURL for this requires bypassing its default behavior of trying to parse your input as form data or JSON.

The most direct way to achieve this without manually constructing the entire HTTP header block is to configure the cURL handle to treat your string as the raw body content. You can pass options via `curl_setopt()` to set the post mode, define the content type, and provide the data string directly.

To implement this, you initialize a standard cURL session and set the URL. Crucially, you must disable the automatic reading of POST fields from the request body. By default, if you pass a string to `CURLOPT_POST`, cURL might interpret it as a form field value depending on other settings. To prevent this, ensure you are setting the post data explicitly or relying on the fact that no standard fields are being read.

Here is how you configure the handle:

```php
$handle = curl_init('https://example.com/api/raw');

// Set the request method to POST
curl_setopt($handle, CURLOPT_POST, true);

// Disable reading post fields from the file (not applicable here as we use a string)
// but ensure we don't try to parse the body as JSON or form data automatically.
// The key is simply passing the raw string in the correct option.

// Set the content type explicitly as requested
curl_setopt($handle, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, [
    'Content-Type: text/plain',
    'Content-Length: 1039' // Note: cURL calculates this automatically if you don't set it manually below
]);

// The raw data string. Do NOT use curl_setopt(CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $data) if you want to control headers strictly,
// because curl_setopt(CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $data) often interferes with header generation unless configured carefully.
// Instead, use CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS with a string, which is the standard way to send raw body content.
$rawData = "89c5fdataasdhf kajshfd akjshfksa hfdkjsa falkjshfsa\najshd fkjsahfd lkjsahflksahfdlkashfhsadkjfsalhfd\najshdfhsafiahfiuwhflsf this is just data from a string\nmore data kjahfdhsakjfhsalkjfdhalksfd";

// Set the post fields. This tells cURL to send this exact string as the body.
curl_setopt($handle, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $rawData);

// Ensure we don't automatically parse the body
curl_setopt($handle, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, [
    'Content-Type: text/plain',
]);

// Execute the request
$response = curl_exec($handle);
$httpCode = curl_getinfo($handle, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE);
curl_close($handle);
```

The critical decision here is using `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` with a string argument. When you pass a string to this option, cURL treats it as the raw request body. It automatically calculates and sets the `Content-Length` header based on the byte size of that string, which handles the requirement for the specific length header shown in your example without needing manual manipulation.

However, there is a significant trade-off regarding headers. If you need to send custom headers beyond `Content-Type` (such as custom authentication tokens or specific raw headers like `X-Custom-Header`) while sending raw data, you must use the `CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER` option alongside `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS`. It is important not to manually write the full HTTP header block in the code unless you are using a low-level socket approach; cURL handles the status line and length calculation internally if configured correctly.

One potential pitfall is mixing `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` with form data arrays. If your `$rawData` string happens to contain characters that look like form parameters, cURL will not misinterpret them as fields because it sees a single string value, not an associative array. This ensures the payload remains raw and unencoded.

For projects built on the Laravel framework, managing raw HTTP requests often benefits from using the `Http` facade or Guzzle, but native cURL offers direct control over these low-level details. If you require advanced features like streaming large files or complex chunked encoding in a PHP application, consulting resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide insights into modern HTTP handling patterns within the ecosystem. However, for simple raw string transmission, the cURL configuration above remains the most efficient and transparent solution.
